Use Image 1 as the strict character identity, appearance, clothing, composition, lighting, prop and background reference.

Create one continuous fixed-camera popular-science presentation. The uploaded source audio is the exact final speech and lip-sync source. Preserve the original words, voice, timing, pacing, pauses and emotion of the uploaded audio. The character speaks directly to the camera with accurate and natural lip synchronization.

The camera remains completely locked throughout the video. Preserve the original medium-shot framing, focal length, perspective, character scale and background. No cuts, no zoom, no push-in, no pull-out, no camera shake, no close-up, no cropping and no reframing.

The fixed camera must not make the character look frozen. The character gives a natural, engaging and professional educational explanation, with upper-body movement and hand gestures synchronized with the rhythm, emphasis and emotional changes of the uploaded speech.

Allow natural conversational body language:
- subtle forward and backward upper-body shifts;
- gentle shoulder movement caused by breathing and speaking;
- occasional small head nods on important points;
- natural changes in facial expression;
- smooth transitions between relaxed and emphasized posture;
- natural blinking and small eye movements;
- small-to-medium explanatory hand gestures;
- hands moving naturally from the elbows and wrists;
- fingers opening and closing naturally during emphasis;
- gestures returning smoothly to a relaxed position after each point.

The hand that is not holding the product may perform natural explanatory gestures, including a gentle open-palm gesture, a small pointing gesture and a soft emphasis gesture. The gestures must be varied, purposeful and synchronized with the speech rather than repetitive.

The hand holding the product may move slightly with the upper body, gently lift or rotate by a small amount, but must maintain a secure and natural grip. Preserve the product's shape, size, color, label, orientation and position. Do not let the product disappear, bend, melt, duplicate or change appearance.

Keep both hands visible whenever possible. Maintain anatomically correct hands, five natural fingers per hand, stable wrists and smooth motion. Hands must not pass in front of the face or cover the mouth.

Movement intensity should be moderate and realistic. The character should feel alive and engaged, but not exaggerated. Avoid large body sway, rapid arm movement, repeated waving, mechanical gestures, frozen shoulders, stationary hands, sudden pose changes and theatrical performance.

Preserve the character's facial identity throughout the video. Maintain realistic skin texture, natural eyes and teeth, stable facial proportions, consistent hair, clothing, lighting and background. No plastic skin, no identity drift, no facial warping, no oversized mouth, no deformed teeth, no extra fingers, no fused fingers, no broken wrists and no product deformation.

Professional, approachable and natural popular-science presenter style. Realistic live-action motion, stable temporal consistency and clear educational-video quality.

